A||||4||Mark||head gasket - which end is up?||||5||
Z||000000||Mark||11-26-2000||12:48 AM||||The Haynes manual states that the V6 head gasket should be installed on the block with "This Side Up" visible.  The Fel-Pro gasket I just bought has no such marking.  The gasket looks pretty much the same on both sides, except that the three metal cylinder rings are bridged together on one side only.  Fel-Pro says nothing about which side should face up.  Does it matter?  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Mark||11-26-2000||05:39 PM||||Thanks, but I figured it out.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||11-26-2000||07:58 PM||||Mind if I ask how you figured it out? I had the same problem and I just decided to stick it in there and hope I got it right. But, I am having to re-do the head gaskets because a damn spark plug peice fell into the intake manifold. Figure I should do it right this time since I will have to get a new gaskets anyway. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"If you smoke after sex, you're doing it way to fast".</I>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mark||11-26-2000||11:16 PM||||I dug the old gasket out of the trash.  The impressions from the water jacket holes were clearly visible on the bottom (block side) of the gasket.  Also, there is a small difference in the shape of the water jacket holes at the two ends of the block. Using these impressions, I determined which way the old gasket was installed.  The new Fel Pro gasket lookes exactly like the old one. <P>Bottom line is the side of the gasket with all the writing (logo, part number, etc) is the top (head side).  The side of the gasket that has the metal "bridges" between the cylinder rings goes against the block.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||11-27-2000||11:54 AM||||Seanh,<P>DON'T PULL THE HEADS OFF AGAIN!<BR>Instead just go to down to removing the upper and lower plenum. Then use a vacumn to remove the lost 'piece of spark plug' from the cylinder. (use a shop vac that's empty to see part.)||||reg||1
